{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run inventory-api on node1 as an active managed service.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor created a dedicated systemd service and account. The service was enabled, active, listening on port 8080, and returned valid health and inventory responses. Its enabled and healthy state persisted after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3112a8e21b8a4715","cmd-7f7a2ba9246f4e97","cmd-efd36c037c8a49d3","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep inventory-api serving while a memory-hungry process drives the system into an out-of-memory event.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy baseline was captured before pressure. During the bounded global OOM event, the availability monitor completed 400 requests with 400 successes and no failures; immediately afterward, inventory-api remained active, healthy, and on the same PID. The service also remained healthy after the test.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6895d4dc99ad48ca","cmd-162ee6c43f184d74","cmd-8e4d69b03493451f"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Ensure the memory hog is the process stopped by the kernel during the OOM event.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The named memory-hog process was observed allocating memory, and kernel logs directly identify PID 1606 as the process killed during a global OOM with oom_score_adj=1000. Its transient service ended with result oom-kill while inventory-api survived.","evidence_ids":["cmd-42fc2dbd74c44304","cmd-162ee6c43f184d74"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the account, application, unit, and OOM protections was task-directed (cmd-3112a8e21b8a4715, cmd-d50c968b5a4d42a7). The temporary monitor and memory-hog artifacts were necessary for the bounded behavior test and were removed afterward (cmd-6895d4dc99ad48ca, cmd-42fc2dbd74c44304, cmd-a03f5491a83e425c). The required node-restart protocol was complete: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service and endpoint state; the lifecycle boot observations independently agree. Final snapshots show no failed services or damaged package state. The new port 8080 listener and service-private temporary directory are expected task state, not residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3112a8e21b8a4715","cmd-d50c968b5a4d42a7","cmd-6895d4dc99ad48ca","cmd-42fc2dbd74c44304","cmd-a03f5491a83e425c","cmd-27ad83f3b3144acc","cmd-efd36c037c8a49d3","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. inventory-api was installed as a managed service, remained continuously responsive through a demonstrated global OOM event, and the kernel killed the designated memory hog. The independent restart protocol also coherently demonstrates persistence and recovery.","limitations":[]}
